{
  "dom_id": "step-q94152",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z\\ge 1.56).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9265\\,$ | $\\,0.9279\\,$ | $\\,0.9292\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9394\\,$ | $\\,0.9406\\,$ | $\\,0.9418\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "We're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z\\ge 1.56).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14254773/assets/c59a56406bcd9bd54e34c2bbeffca20ae0633d654a4c69f32aca3569e9ad2f20.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nSince the total area under the graph equals $1,$ we can express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 1.56) & =1-P(Z<1.56) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.56)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14254773/assets/eb5bf827193cff372f78486d5e2ca26c63a6e310f415ffcc0b91539e79c7a566.png)\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.56)=0.9406.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 1.56) & =1-Φ(1.56) \\\\  & =1-0.9406 \\\\  & =0.0594.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
